way.\n\nPoint 5: All nice things are big. So this is a general rule. So any nice thing must be big. So since Dave is nice, he must be big.\n\nPoint 6: All furry things are blue. So if something is furry, it's blue. Erin is furry and blue, which fits.\n\nPoint 7: Blue and furry things are quiet. Erin is blue, furry, and quiet, so that's consistent.\n\nPoint 8: If something is round and nice, then it's blue. Erin is round and... is she nice? The context doesn't say Erin is nice, so this rule doesn't apply to her. But if someone is round and nice, they must be blue.\n\nPoint 9: If Bob is round, then he's blue. Bob is big, nice, and smart. The context doesn't say Bob is round, so this rule doesn't necessarily apply unless we know Bob is round.\n\nPoint 10: Big and quiet things are furry. Erin is big, quiet, and furry, so that fits.\n\nPoint 11: If something is blue and furry, then it's smart. Erin is blue, furry, and smart, so that's consistent.\n\nPoint 12: If Charlie is nice and big, then he's quiet. Since Charlie is nice, he's big (from point 5), so he must be quiet. That's consistent with point 10, as big and quiet things are furry, so Charlie would also be furry? Wait, but the context doesn't say Charlie is furry. Hmm, maybe that's an implication. Let me think.\n\nWait, if Charlie is big and quiet, then from point 10, he must be furry. So Charlie is nice, big, quiet, and furry. But the context only says Charlie is nice. So does that mean Charlie is also furry? Because if he's big and quiet, he must be furry. But the context doesn't mention Charlie being furry, so maybe that's an implication.\n\nBut back to Dave. Dave is nice, so he must be big. There's no information that contradicts this. So the statement \"Dave is big\" should be true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure there's no rule that could make Dave not big despite being nice. All the rules seem to support that nice things are big. So unless there's a rule that says something else, Dave must be big.\n\nI think 