ave is big, then he would be white. If he's not big, then we don't know his color from this statement.\n\nWait, but let's see if we can determine if Dave is big. From statement 10, if something is big and white, then it's round. But we don't know if Dave is big or white yet. Alternatively, from statement 14, quiet things are big. Since all nice things are quiet (statement 13), and Dave is nice, he must be quiet. Therefore, Dave is quiet, which from statement 14 means he's big. So Dave is big.\n\nNow that we know Dave is big, let's go back to statement 9: all nice, big things are white. Since Dave is nice and big, he must be white. So Dave is white.\n\nNow, let's check if there's any contradiction or additional info about Dave's color. From statement 15, young, quiet things are red. But Dave is round, which makes him nice, and we've established he's big and white. Is Dave young? Let's check. From statement 3, Bob is young, and statement 7, Gary is young. There's no direct statement about Dave's age, but from statement 11, young things are nice, which Dave is, but that doesn't necessarily mean he's young. So Dave could be young or not.\n\nWait, but if Dave were young, then from statement 15, since he's quiet (as he's nice), he would have to be red. But we've already concluded he's white. So that would be a contradiction. Therefore, Dave cannot be young because if he were, he'd have to be red, but he's white. So Dave is not young.\n\nSo, Dave is round, nice, big, white, and not young. Now, is there any statement that links Dave's color to red? From statement 15, only young, quiet things are red. Since Dave is not young, he doesn't fall into that category. Therefore, Dave's color is white, so he is not red.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Erin is big, red, and young. Gary is young, quiet, and red. So red is associated with being young and quiet. Since Dave is not young, he can't be red. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not red\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct opt