\n- Dave is white → young (point 14). Correct.\n- Dave is smart → green (point 11). Correct.\n- Dave is green → nice (point 12). Correct.\n\nSo, all the implications chain together without any contradictions. Therefore, Dave must be nice.\n\nI don\'t see any conflicting information. For example, there\'s a point about Fiona not being red (point 7), but that doesn\'t affect Dave. Similarly, Gary is cold and red (points 9 and 10), but that\'s separate from Dave.\n\nAnother point is that all young, red people are smart (point 17). Since Dave is young (from being white) but not red (he\'s white), this doesn\'t apply to him. So, no issue there.\n\nAlso, point 16 says if someone is green and smart, then they are white. Dave is green and smart, so he must be white, which he is. So, that\'s consistent.\n\nPoint 18 is about Fiona: if she\'s red, then she\'s green. But Fiona isn\'t red (point 7), so that doesn\'t affect her or Dave.\n\nPoint 19 is about Anne: if she\'s smart, then she\'s not green. But Anne is nice and young (points 1 and 2). If Anne were smart, she would be green (from point 11), but point 19 says she wouldn\'t be. So, that suggests Anne isn\'t smart. But that\'s about Anne, not Dave.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Dave is cold → white → young, smart → green → nice. Therefore, Dave is nice. The statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A', '']
