ake her blue.\n\nFact 11: All blue, furry things are big. Anne is furry, but we don't know if she's blue. If she were blue, then she would be big, which she is, so that's consistent.\n\nFact 12: If Gary is furry, then Gary is blue. But Gary is smart (fact 7), and all smart things are furry (fact 8), so Gary is furry and therefore blue (from fact 12). Then, since Gary is blue and furry, he must be big (fact 11). But Gary's properties don't directly affect Anne.\n\nFact 10: If Gary is cold, then Gary is smart. But Gary is already smart, so if he were cold, it would confirm his smartness, but since he's smart, he must be furry (fact 8). But again, this doesn't affect Anne.\n\nSo, going back to Anne, all the facts about her are consistent. She is cold, which is directly stated. There's no information that contradicts this. Therefore, the statement \"Anne is cold\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not missing any indirect contradictions. For example, if being cold implies something else that might conflict with her other properties. Fact 14 says cold, white things are furry, which Anne is, so that's fine. Fact 13 says round things are cold, but Anne isn't round, so that's okay too.\n\nI don't see any contradictions. So, the answer should be true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Anne is cold\" is directly supported by the given information. There are no contradictions in the facts provided that would make this statement false. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
