tatement 14, he's white. From statement 9, he's green. From statement 8, green and furry implies smart. So, that suggests Charlie is smart.\n\nBut then, from statement 11, cold and smart implies red. But Charlie is white, not red. So, there's a contradiction here. How can Charlie be both white and red?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake. Let me go through it again.\n\nCharlie is furry (statement 2) \u2192 cold (statement 12). Charlie is kind (statement 3). So, cold and kind \u2192 white (statement 14). So, Charlie is white.\n\nIf Charlie is white, then from statement 9, he's green. So, Charlie is green, furry, and kind. From statement 8, green and furry \u2192 smart. So, Charlie is smart.\n\nBut then, from statement 11: cold and smart \u2192 red. But Charlie is white, not red. So, this seems conflicting.\n\nIs there a way to resolve this? Maybe the statements are consistent, and I'm just misapplying them.\n\nWait, statement 11 says cold and smart things are red. So, if Charlie is cold and smart, he must be red. But we also have that Charlie is white. So, can Charlie be both red and white? Or is that impossible?\n\nLooking back, statement 7 says Gary is red and white. So, it's possible for something to be both red and white. So, maybe Charlie can be both red and white.\n\nWait, but in the given information, Charlie is white (from statement 14) and if he's smart, he would be red (from statement 11). So, Charlie could be both red and white. That's allowed because Gary is both red and white.\n\nSo, that resolves the contradiction. Therefore, Charlie can be both red and white, so being smart doesn't conflict with being white.\n\nTherefore, Charlie is smart.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's another way. Maybe Charlie isn't smart. Let's see.\n\nIf Charlie isn't smart, then from statement 8, since he's green and furry, he should be smart. So, that would mean Charlie is smart. So, it's not possible for Charlie to not be smart.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie isn't smart, then 