n, rough, and white. Dave is blue and white. So Anne is blue, so according to rule 6, she must be green. But the initial facts don\'t say Anne is green. That\'s a problem. Maybe the rules take precedence over the initial facts? Or perhaps the initial facts are correct, and the rules are additional constraints. So maybe Anne is both blue and green because rule 6 says all blue people are green. So Anne is blue, so she must be green. But the initial facts don\'t mention her being green, so perhaps that\'s an oversight, or maybe the rules are more important.\n\n7. If someone is young and white, then they are big. Harry is young, and from rule 2, he must be white. So according to this rule, Harry should be big. That seems to answer the question directly. So Harry is young and white, so he must be big. Therefore, the statement "Harry is big" is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Harry is young, so he\'s white (rule 2). Then, since he\'s young and white, rule 7 says he must be big. So yes, Harry is big. So the answer should be A) True.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn\'t miss anything. Are there any contradictions or other rules that might affect this? For example, does being big affect other attributes? Anne is big, blue, and rough. Bob is cold, green, rough, and white. Dave is blue and white. So big people can be blue or white. Harry being big would make him white (from rule 7), and since he\'s white and big, rule 5 says he must be blue. But the initial facts don\'t say Harry is blue, but according to rule 5, if he\'s white and big, he must be blue. So Harry would be big, white, and blue. But the initial facts don\'t mention Harry\'s color, only that he\'s young. So according to the rules, Harry must be big, white, and blue. But the question is only about whether he\'s big, so the answer is true.\n\nI think that\'s solid. So the correct answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nHarry is young, so according to the rule, he must be white. Since h