t, they must be nice. Since Dave is not nice, he can\'t be smart. So, Dave is not smart.\n\nNow, point 16 says if someone is quiet and smart, they are round. But since Dave isn\'t smart, this doesn\'t apply to him. \n\nPoint 8 and 9 say that all round people are quiet and green. But again, since we don\'t know if Dave is round, but from point 11, he\'s furry, so he\'s green. \n\nWait, but point 10 says quiet, furry people are green. So, does that mean that if a furry person is quiet, they are green, but if they\'re not quiet, maybe they aren\'t? But point 11 says all furry people are green, regardless of being quiet or not. So, point 11 overrides point 10 in this case because it\'s a more general statement.\n\nTherefore, since Dave is furry, he must be green. So, the statement "Dave is green" is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any way that Dave could not be green? If point 11 is a general rule, then yes, all furry people are green. So, Dave being furry makes him green. There\'s no exception given for furry people not being green unless they\'re not quiet, but point 11 doesn\'t have that condition. So, I think the answer is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A', '']
