      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement \"Gary is big\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given information. Let me start by listing out all the facts provided and see how they connect.\n\nFirst, let's list all the given statements:\n\n1. Anne is big.\n2. Anne is cold.\n3. Anne is furry.\n4. Anne is white.\n5. Erin is cold.\n6. Fiona is round.\n7. Gary is smart.\n8. All smart things are furry.\n9. Big, blue things are round.\n10. If Gary is cold then Gary is smart.\n11. All blue, furry things are big.\n12. If Gary is furry then Gary is blue.\n13. Round things are cold.\n14. Cold, white things are furry.\n15. Round things are blue.\n\nNow, the question is about Gary. Let's focus on the information related to Gary.\n\nFrom statement 7, we know Gary is smart. Then, statement 8 says all smart things are furry. So, since Gary is smart, he must be furry. So, Gary is furry.\n\nNow, looking at statement 12: If Gary is furry, then he is blue. Since we've established Gary is furry, this means Gary is blue.\n\nNext, statement 11 says all blue, furry things are big. We know Gary is both blue and furry, so according to this, Gary must be big. So, that would make the statement \"Gary is big\" true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check to make sure I didn't miss anything. Let's go through each step again.\n\n1. Gary is smart (statement 7).\n2. All smart things are furry (statement 8), so Gary is furry.\n3. If Gary is furry, then he is blue (statement 12), so Gary is blue.\n4. All blue, furry things are big (statement 11), so Gary is big.\n\nThat seems solid. But let me check if there are any conflicting statements. For example, does anything say Gary isn't big? I don't see any such statement. All the connections point towards Gary being big.\n\nAnother angle: Let's see if any other properties of Gary could affect this. We know Gary is smart, which leads to furry, which leads to blue, which with furry makes him big. There's no contradiction here.\n\nWait, what about statement 9: Big, blue things are round. So, if Gary is big and blue, he should be round. Does that affect anything? Well, being round is another property, but it doesn't contradict being big. So, Gary would be big, blue, and round.\n\nAlso, statement 13 says round things are cold. So, if Gary is round, he must be cold. But statement 10 says if Gary is cold, then he is smart. Well, we already know Gary is smart, so that's consistent. So, Gary being cold is another property, but it doesn't affect whether he's big.\n\nWait, but does being cold affect anything else? Statement 14 says cold, white things are furry. But we don't know if Gary is white. Anne is white, but that's separate. So, Gary's coldness doesn't add any new info about being big.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the chain of reasoning leads us to conclude that Gary is big. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
