final_output:  ['Alright, let\'s try to figure out whether the statement "The bald eagle likes the rabbit" is true, false, or unknown based on the given information. I\'ll go through each statement one by one and see what conclusions I can draw.\n\nFirst, let\'s list out all the given information:\n\n1. The bald eagle is big.\n2. The bald eagle likes the cow.\n3. The bald eagle sees the rabbit.\n4. The cow is big.\n5. The cow sees the bald eagle.\n6. The dog is big.\n7. The dog is young.\n8. The dog likes the bald eagle.\n9. The rabbit sees the bald eagle.\n10. The rabbit visits the bald eagle.\n11. If the bald eagle sees the rabbit, then the rabbit is big.\n12. If something likes the bald eagle, then it is blue.\n13. If something likes the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow, then the cow is young.\n14. If something visits the rabbit, then it is round.\n15. If something is round and it sees the cow, then the cow sees the rabbit.\n16. If something is big, then it visits the cow.\n17. If something sees the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the bald eagle.\n18. If something is blue, then it likes the rabbit.\n19. If something likes the cow and the cow is young, then it is blue.\n\nNow, let\'s break down each point and see what we can infer.\n\nStarting with statement 3: The bald eagle sees the rabbit. From statement 11, if the bald eagle sees the rabbit, then the rabbit is big. So, the rabbit must be big.\n\nStatement 10 says the rabbit visits the bald eagle. From statement 14, if something visits the rabbit, then it is round. Wait, but statement 10 is about the rabbit visiting the bald eagle, not the other way around. So, does that mean the bald eagle is round? Because the rabbit is visiting the bald eagle, so the bald eagle would be the one being visited. Hmm, maybe I\'m getting this wrong. Let me think again.\n\nStatement 14 says if something visits the rabbit, then it is round. So, if the rabbit is visited by someone, that someone is round. But in statement 10, the rabbit visits the bald eagle, so the rabbit is the one visiting, not the other way around. So, does that mean the rabbit is round? Because the rabbit is visiting the bald eagle, so the rabbit would be the one visiting, making the rabbit round? Or is it the other way around?\n\nWait, no. Statement 14 says if something visits the rabbit, then it is round. So, if X visits the rabbit, then X is round. But in statement 10, the rabbit visits the bald eagle, so the rabbit is visiting the bald eagle. That doesn\'t directly trigger statement 14 because it\'s the rabbit visiting someone else, not being visited. So, maybe statement 14 doesn\'t apply here. Hmm, maybe I need to look elsewhere.\n\nLooking at statement 16: If something is big, then it visits the cow. The bald eagle is big (statement 1), so the bald eagle visits the cow. So, the bald eagle visits the cow.\n\nFrom statement 5: The cow sees the bald eagle. So, the cow sees the bald eagle, and from statement 16, the bald eagle visits the cow. So, the bald eagle and the cow have a mutual interaction.\n\nNow, statement 17: If something sees the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the bald eagle. The rabbit sees the bald eagle (statement 9), so according to statement 17, the rabbit visits the bald eagle. Which is consistent with statement 10.\n\nNow, let\'s look at statement 12: If something likes the bald eagle, then it is blue. The dog likes the bald eagle (statement 8), so the dog is blue.\n\nFrom statement 18: If something is blue, then it likes the rabbit. Since the dog is blue, the dog likes the rabbit.\n\nNow, statement 13: If something likes the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow, then the cow is young. We know the dog likes the rabbit, so if the rabbit visits the cow, then the cow is young. But does the rabbit visit the cow? Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 10: The rabbit visits the bald eagle. From statement 16: The bald eagle visits the cow. So, the rabbit is visiting the bald eagle, who is visiting the cow. Does that mean the rabbit visits the cow indirectly? Or does the rabbit need to directly visit the cow for statement 13 to apply?\n\nStatement 13 says if something likes the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow, then the cow is young. So, the rabbit needs to visit the cow for this to apply. But from the given information, the rabbit visits the bald eagle, not the cow. So, unless the rabbit also visits the cow, statement 13 doesn\'t apply. Therefore, we don\'t know if the cow is young based on this.\n\nWait, but statement 16 says if something is big, it visits the cow. The rabbit is big (from statement 11, since the bald eagle sees the rabbit, so the rabbit is big). So, the rabbit is big, so the rabbit visits the cow. Therefore, the rabbit visits the cow.\n\nSo, now, since the rabbit visits the cow, and the dog likes the rabbit (from earlier), then according to statement 13, the cow is young.\n\nBut wait, statement 4 says the cow is big. So, the cow is big and now we\'re concluding it\'s young. That\'s possible, as an animal can be both big and young.\n\nNow, statement 19: If something likes the cow and the cow is young, then it is blue. We know the cow is young (from statement 13), and from statement 2, the bald eagle likes the cow. So, the bald eagle likes the cow and the cow is young, so according to statement 19, the bald eagle is blue.\n\nFrom statement 18: If something is blue, then it likes the rabbit. Since the bald eagle is blue, the bald eagle likes the rabbit.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Bald eagle is big (1) → visits cow (16)\n- Bald eagle sees rabbit (3) → rabbit is big (11)\n- Rabbit is big → visits cow (16)\n- Dog likes bald eagle (8) → dog is blue (12)\n- Dog is blue → likes rabbit (18)\n- Dog likes rabbit and rabbit visits cow (from above) → cow is young (13)\n- Cow is young and bald eagle likes cow (2) → bald eagle is blue (19)\n- Bald eagle is blue → likes rabbit (18)\n\nTherefore, the bald eagle likes the rabbit. So, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A', ''] 22%|##1       | 129/600 [50:59<2:26:01, 18.60s/it] 22%|##1       | 130/600 [51:00<1:44:51, 13.39s/it]
