g to statement 16, means the rabbit also visits the rabbit? Wait, that seems a bit confusing. Let me think again.\n\nWait, statement 16 says: If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit. So, the rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), so according to statement 16, the rabbit must also visit the rabbit. That seems a bit circular, but perhaps it\'s just reinforcing that the rabbit visits the cat, which in turn visits the rabbit. But does that mean the rabbit visits the bald eagle?\n\nWait, the cat visits the bald eagle (statement 3). So, if the rabbit visits the cat, does that mean the rabbit also visits the bald eagle? Let me check statement 16 again. It says if something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit. So, the rabbit visits the cat, so it must visit the rabbit. But that doesn\'t directly imply that the rabbit visits the bald eagle. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I should look at statement 17: If something visits the rabbit and it sees the cow, then it visits the cow. The rabbit sees the cow (statement 9), so if something visits the rabbit and sees the cow, it visits the cow. But the rabbit itself visits the cat, which is statement 10. So, the rabbit visits the cat, which in turn visits the rabbit (statement 4). So, the cat visits the rabbit, and the rabbit visits the cat. But does that chain lead to the rabbit visiting the bald eagle?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the cat visits the bald eagle, and the rabbit visits the cat. So, does that mean the rabbit indirectly visits the bald eagle? Or does visiting the cat not necessarily mean visiting the bald eagle?\n\nWait, statement 13 says that if something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice. So, if the rabbit visits the bald eagle, it\'s nice, hence big. But does the rabbit visit the bald eagle?\n\nLooking back, the cat visits the bald eagle (statement 3). The rabbit visits the cat (statement 10). So, does visiting the cat imply visiting the bald eagle? Not directly, unless there\'s a rule that says if you visit