 that mean the bald eagle sees the dog? Not necessarily. The statement says \"likes,\" not \"sees.\" So, maybe the mouse likes the dog (statement 6), but the mouse does not see the dog (statement 7). So, the mouse doesn't see the dog, but does the dog see the mouse? Not sure yet.\n\nWait, statement 13 says if someone sees the dog, then they need the mouse. So, if someone sees the dog, they need the mouse. But who sees the dog? The mouse doesn't see the dog (statement 7). The bald eagle likes the dog, but does that imply seeing? Not necessarily. Maybe the lion needs the dog (statement 4), but does the lion see the dog? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog is seen by someone else. Let's think about the lion. The lion does not need the bald eagle (statement 5). The dog needs the lion (statement 4). So, the dog needs the lion, which means according to statement 9, if someone needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion. Wait, statement 9 is about needing the dog, not the other way around. So, if someone needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion. But the dog does need the lion (statement 4), so that condition is satisfied.\n\nBut back to statement 13: If someone sees the dog, then they need the mouse. So, if someone sees the dog, they need the mouse. But who sees the dog? The mouse doesn't (statement 7). The bald eagle likes the dog, but does that mean the bald eagle sees the dog? Not necessarily. Maybe the lion sees the dog? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog is seen by someone else, but without more info, it's unclear. So, perhaps the condition in statement 13 isn't triggered because no one sees the dog. Therefore, maybe no one needs the mouse because of that.\n\nWait, but the mouse likes the dog (statement 6), but doesn't see the dog (statement 7). So, the mouse's liking the dog doesn't imply seeing.\n\nNow, going back to the bald eagle. Since the bald eagle is rough (from statements 1 and 14), statement 10 says they see the mouse. So, that would mean the bald eagle sees the mouse, making the statement true.\n\nBut wait, let's check statement 8 again: If someone needs the mouse, then the mouse does not see the bald eagle. So, if someone needs the mouse, the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. But does anyone need the mouse? From statement 13, if someone sees the dog, they need the mouse. But if no one sees the dog, then no one needs the mouse. So, if no one needs the mouse, then statement 8 doesn't come into play. Therefore, the mouse could see the bald eagle or not.\n\nWait, but the bald eagle is rough, so according to statement 10, the bald eagle sees the mouse. So, the bald eagle sees the mouse, which would mean the mouse is seen by the bald eagle. But does that affect the mouse's ability to see the bald eagle? Not necessarily. The mouse could see the bald eagle or not, but statement 8 only says that if someone needs the mouse, then the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. Since no one needs the mouse (because no one sees the dog, as per statement 7 and 13), then the condition in statement 8 isn't triggered. Therefore, the mouse could see the bald eagle or not, but the bald eagle definitely sees the mouse because they're rough.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the bald eagle sees the mouse. From statement 10, since the bald eagle is rough, they see the mouse. So, the statement should be true.\n\nBut let me double-check. Is there any contradiction? If the bald eagle sees the mouse, then according to statement 11, the mouse is kind. Is the mouse kind? From statement 11, if someone sees the mouse, then the mouse is kind. So, if the bald eagle sees the mouse, then the mouse is kind. But is there any info about the mouse's kindness? Not directly, but if the mouse is kind, that's fine. There's no contradiction here.\n\nAlso, statement 12 says if someone needs the lion, they don't see the bald eagle. The lion does not need the bald eagle (statement 5), so that doesn't affect the bald eagle's ability to see the mouse.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the bald eagle is rough, so they see the mouse. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
