es not eat the lion. So, the bear and lion have some interaction, but the bear doesn\'t eat the lion.\n\nFrom statement 5: The lion does not eat the bald eagle. So, the lion and bald eagle don\'t eat each other.\n\nFrom statement 11: If the bald eagle is round, then it chases the lion. So, if the bald eagle is round, it chases the lion. But does the bald eagle chase the lion? Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 14: If something chases the lion, then it is blue. So, if the bald eagle chases the lion, it must be blue.\n\nFrom statement 12: If something is blue, then it chases the squirrel. So, if the bald eagle is blue, it chases the squirrel, which it does (statement 1). So, that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, let\'s try to determine the color and shape of the bald eagle.\n\nFrom statement 15: Blue, red things are round. The squirrel is blue and red, so it\'s round. The bald eagle\'s color isn\'t directly given, but let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 12: If something is blue, it chases the squirrel. The bald eagle chases the squirrel, so if it\'s blue, that fits. But is the bald eagle blue? Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 13: All red things are blue. The squirrel is red, so it\'s blue. The bear\'s color isn\'t mentioned, but the lion\'s color isn\'t either.\n\nFrom statement 14: If something chases the lion, it\'s blue. So, if the bald eagle chases the lion, it must be blue. But does the bald eagle chase the lion? From statement 11: If the bald eagle is round, then it chases the lion. So, if the bald eagle is round, it chases the lion, which would make it blue (from statement 14). But does the bald eagle chase the lion? Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 16: If something visits the bald eagle and is not round, it visits the lion. The squirrel visits the bald eagle and is round, so it doesn\'t necessarily visit the lion. The bear visits the lion (statement 4), but does the bear visit the bald eagle? Not directly stated, but let\'s see.\n\nWait, the bear visits the lion (statement 4), but does the bear visit the bald eagle? Not mentioned. So, perhaps the only visitor to the bald eagle is the squirrel.\n\nNow, let\'s try to figure out if the bald eagle is round. If the bald eagle is round, then from statement 11, it chases the lion. If it chases the lion, from statement 14, it must be blue. If it\'s blue, from statement 12, it chases the squirrel, which it does. So, that\'s consistent.\n\nBut is the bald eagle round? Let\'s see. From statement 15: Blue, red things are round. The squirrel is blue and red, so it\'s round. The bald eagle\'s color isn\'t directly given, but if it\'s blue, then perhaps it\'s round? Or maybe not. Let\'s see.\n\nWait, statement 15 says blue and red things are round. So, if something is both blue and red, it\'s round. The squirrel is both, so it\'s round. The bald eagle\'s color isn\'t specified, but if it\'s blue, does that make it round? Not necessarily, unless it\'s also red. But the bald eagle\'s color isn\'t given as red. So, perhaps the bald eagle is only blue, not red, so it\'s not necessarily round.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle is red. Let\'s see. From statement 13: All red things are blue. So, if the bald eagle is red, it must be blue. But we don\'t know if it\'s red.\n\nWait, the squirrel is red, so it\'s blue. The bear\'s color isn\'t mentioned. The lion\'s color isn\'t mentioned either.\n\nLet me try to see if the bald eagle is round. If the bald eagle is round, then from statement 11, it chases the lion. If it chases the lion, from statement 14, it must be blue. So, if the bald eagle is round, it\'s blue and chases the lion.\n\nBut does the bald eagle chase the lion? Let\'s see. From statement 1: The bald eagle chases the squirrel. From statement 11, if it\'s round, it chases the lion. So, if it\'s round, it chases both the squirrel and the lion. But we don\'t have any information that the bald eagle chases the lion, except if it\'s round.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle is not round, so it doesn\'t chase the lion. But then, from statement 16, if something visits the bald eagle and is not round, it visits the lion. The squirrel visits the bald eagle and is round, so it doesn\'t necessarily visit the lion. The bear visits the lion, but does the bear visit the bald eagle? Not mentioned.\n\nWait, the bear visits the lion (statement 4), but does the bear visit the bald eagle? Not directly stated. So, perhaps the only visitor to the bald eagle is the squirrel, which is round, so the condition in statement 16 doesn\'t apply.\n\nNow, let\'s go back to the squirrel. The squirrel is red, blue, and round. From statement 10: If something eats the squirrel, then the squirrel is green. But the squirrel is red, so if something eats it, the squirrel would have to be green, which contradicts it being red. Therefore, nothing can eat the squirrel. But from statement 8: If something is round, it eats the squirrel. The squirrel is round, so according to this, the squirrel eats itself, which is impossible. Therefore, there must be a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, perhaps the squirrel is not round. But statement 15 says blue and red things are round, and the squirrel is both, so it must be round. So, the squirrel is round, which means it eats itself, which is impossible. Therefore, the only way this makes sense is if the squirrel is not round, but that contradicts statement 15. Hmm, this is a paradox.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is not round because it\'s red and blue, but statement 15 says blue and red things are round, so it must be round. Therefore, the squirrel is round, which means it eats itself, which is impossible. Therefore, the only way this works is if the squirrel is not round, but that contradicts statement 15. So, perhaps the initial assumption is wrong, and the squirrel is not red? But statement 6 says the squirrel is red, so that can\'t be.\n\nWait, maybe the squirrel is red and blue, but not round. But statement 15 says blue and red things are round, so that can\'t be. Therefore, the squirrel must be round, which leads to a contradiction because it would have to eat itself. Therefore, perhaps the only way out is that the squirrel is not round, but that contradicts statement 15. So, maybe the problem is designed to have this contradiction, but I need to proceed.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel is round, and the fact that it eats itself is acceptable in this context, even though it\'s illogical. So, moving on.\n\nNow, let\'s try to figure out the lion\'s shape. The question is whether the lion is not round.\n\nFrom statement 14: If something chases the lion, then it is blue. So, if the bald eagle chases the lion, it must be blue. But does the bald eagle chase the lion? From statement 11: If the bald eagle is round, then it chases the lion. So, if the bald eagle is round, it chases the lion, which would make it blue (from statement 14). If the bald eagle is blue, from statement 12, it chases the squirrel, which it does.\n\nBut is the bald eagle round? Let\'s see. If the bald eagle is round, then it chases the lion. If it\'s not round, then it doesn\'t chase the lion. But we don\'t have any direct information about the bald eagle\'s shape.\n\nWait, from statement 16: If something visits the bald eagle and is not round, then it visits the lion. The squirrel visits the bald eagle and is round, so the condition doesn\'t apply. The bear visits the lion, but does the bear visit the bald eagle? Not mentioned. So, perhaps the only visitor to the bald eagle is the squirrel, which is round, so the condition in statement 16 doesn\'t apply.\n\nNow, let\'s see if the lion is round or not. From statement 8: If something is round, it eats the squirrel. The squirrel is round, so if the lion is round, it would eat the squirrel. But does the lion eat the squirrel? From statement 5: The lion does not eat the bald eagle. But nothing is said about the lion eating the squirrel. So, if the lion is round, it would eat the squirrel, but