eeds the squirrel. So, the tiger needs the squirrel.\n\nNow, statement 16 says that if something needs the squirrel, it is green. So, the tiger is green.\n\nFrom statement 18, green things are young. So, the tiger is young.\n\nWait, but statement 7 says the squirrel is young, and statement 17 says if something is young, it needs the tiger. So, the squirrel needs the tiger.\n\nBut the squirrel is young, so it needs the tiger. The squirrel also visits the bald eagle (statement 10), which makes the squirrel big (from statement 13). So, the squirrel is big.\n\nNow, statement 20: If the squirrel is big and the squirrel does not like the tiger, then the tiger is not big. But we know the squirrel does like the tiger (statement 8), so the condition in statement 20 is not met. Therefore, we can't conclude anything about the tiger's size from this statement, but we already know the tiger is big from statement 13.\n\nNow, let's see if the tiger needs itself. The statement is \"The tiger does not need the tiger.\" So, does the tiger need itself?\n\nLooking at the rules, I don't see any direct statement about an animal needing itself. The rules talk about needing others, like the squirrel, the tiger, etc. So, unless there's a rule that says an animal doesn't need itself, we can't assume that.\n\nBut let's think about it logically. Generally, in such logic puzzles, unless stated otherwise, an animal doesn't need itself. So, the tiger doesn't need the tiger. Therefore, the statement \"The tiger does not need the tiger\" would be true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any rule that could imply that the tiger needs itself? I don't think so. All the rules are about needing others based on certain conditions. So, unless there's a rule that says an animal needs itself, it's safe to assume it doesn't.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A) True\n\nThe tiger does not need the tiger because there is no rule stating that an animal needs itself