 visits the bald eagle, and the bald eagle visits the squirrel. If both are true, then the bald eagle chases the squirrel.\n\nWe know from statement 4 that the bald eagle visits the squirrel. So, the second part of the condition is satisfied. Now, do we know if someone visits the bald eagle?\n\nLooking through the statements, statement 8 says the squirrel chases the bald eagle. But does that mean the squirrel visits the bald eagle? Not necessarily. Chasing doesn't imply visiting. Visiting is a separate action.\n\nWait, statement 10 says the squirrel visits the tiger. So, the squirrel does visit the tiger. But does the squirrel visit the bald eagle? Statement 8 says the squirrel chases the bald eagle, but not necessarily visits. So, we don't have direct information that the squirrel visits the bald eagle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe someone else visits the bald eagle. Let's see. Statement 4 is about the bald eagle visiting the squirrel, not someone visiting the bald eagle. So, unless another statement tells us that someone visits the bald eagle, the first condition of statement 15 isn't met.\n\nWait, statement 19: If someone chases the lion, then they visit the bald eagle. So, if someone chases the lion, they will visit the bald eagle. Do we know if someone chases the lion?\n\nLooking at statement 11: The tiger is red. Statement 16: If someone is red, then they chase the lion. Since the tiger is red, does that mean the tiger chases the lion? Yes, according to statement 16. So, the tiger chases the lion.\n\nNow, from statement 19: If someone chases the lion, then they visit the bald eagle. Since the tiger chases the lion, the tiger will visit the bald eagle.\n\nSo, the tiger visits the bald eagle. Now, going back to statement 15: If someone visits the bald eagle and the bald eagle visits the squirrel, then the bald eagle chases the squirrel.\n\nWe have both conditions now: someone (the tiger) visits the bald eagle, and the bald eagle visits the squirrel. Therefore, according to statement 15, the bald eagle chases the squirrel.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The tiger visits the bald eagle because the tiger chases the lion (from statement 16 and 19). So, the tiger visits the bald eagle. Then, since the bald eagle visits the squirrel (statement 4), both conditions of statement 15 are met. Therefore, the bald eagle chases the squirrel.\n\nBut wait, statement 8 says the squirrel chases the bald eagle. So, the squirrel is chasing the bald eagle, and according to our deduction, the bald eagle is chasing the squirrel. That seems like a mutual chasing. Is that possible? The statements don't say anything against it, so I think it's allowed.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if there's any contradiction. If the squirrel chases the bald eagle (statement 8), and the bald eagle chases the squirrel (from our deduction), that's just a mutual action. There's no rule against that.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Tiger is red (statement 11) \u2192 Tiger chases lion (statement 16).\n- Tiger chases lion \u2192 Tiger visits bald eagle (statement 19).\n- Tiger visits bald eagle, and bald eagle visits squirrel (statement 4) \u2192 Bald eagle chases squirrel (statement 15).\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The bald eagle chases the squirrel\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
