messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is green. Anne is nice. Anne is not rough. Anne is smart. Bob is green. Bob is nice. Fiona is white. Gary is rough. Gary is round. Gary is white. If something is nice then it is round. If something is white then it is round. If something is young and not nice then it is round. If Bob is round and Bob is green then Bob is rough. If something is young and green then it is smart. Smart things are white. If something is rough and not green then it is white. If something is rough then it is young. All rough things are young.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Fiona is not young.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '.\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**.\n\n1.**Fiona\'s Attributes**: Fiona is white (fact 7).From fact 12, being white means she is round.\n\n2.**Rules Involving Age**:.\n   - Fact 18: If something is rough, it is young.\n   - Fact 19: All rough things are young.\n   - Fact 13: If something is young and not nice, it is round.\n\n3.**Fiona\'s Smoothness**: Fiona\'s smoothness is mentioned. If she were smooth, she would be old (facts 18 & 19). However, her smoothness is known.\n\n4.**Fiona\'s Meanness**: Fiona\'s meanness is mentioned. If she were old and mean, she would be square (fact 13). But since her meanness is known, we can determine her age based on this.\n\n5.**Conclusion**: With information on Fiona\'s smoothness and meanness, we can definitively determine if she is old or not. Therefore, the statement "Fiona is young" is known.']]
