e he's blue, he's young). So Bob is rough and young, so he must be round. Therefore, Bob is round. So Bob is big, blue, rough, smart, and round, and thus young.\n\nCharlie:\n- Charlie is smart (6). From fact 10, smart people are quiet. So Charlie is quiet. Now, from fact 9, if someone is smart and quiet, they are young. Charlie is smart and quiet, so he must be young. So that would make the statement \"Charlie is young\" true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Charlie is smart, so he's quiet (fact 10). Then, since he's smart and quiet, he's young (fact 9). So yes, Charlie is young.\n\nWait, but let me make sure there's no conflicting information. Let's see if Charlie could be not young. Is there any rule that could contradict this? Let's see. All rough, young people are round (fact 14). But Charlie is smart, so from fact 15, he's rough. So Charlie is rough and young, so he must be round (from fact 14). But does that affect his age? No, because being round is a separate attribute. So Charlie is rough, smart, quiet, and young. So the statement is true.\n\nWait, but let me check if Charlie is round. From fact 14, since he's rough and young, he must be round. So Charlie is round. From fact 11, if someone is round, they are blue. So Charlie is blue. From fact 13, if someone is blue, they are young. So that's consistent with what we already concluded.\n\nSo putting it all together, Charlie is smart, quiet, rough, round, blue, and young. Therefore, the statement \"Charlie is young\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
