gs are furry. So Harry is furry.\n\nNow, let's check Gary. Statement 3 says Gary is blue, statement 4 says he's kind, statement 5 says he's quiet, and statement 6 says he's red. Wait, that's interesting. So Gary is both blue and red. Is that possible? Let me see if there's any contradiction.\n\nStatement 10 says if something is green and smart, then it's not blue. But Gary is blue, so if he were green and smart, that would be a problem. But we don't know if Gary is green. So maybe that's okay.\n\nStatement 12 says all blue, green things are smart. So if something is both blue and green, it's smart. But again, we don't know if Gary is green.\n\nSo, back to Fiona. Fiona is quiet. We need to see if she's smart. Let's see if any rules connect quietness to smartness.\n\nStatement 9: Quiet, smart things are blue. So if Fiona were smart, she would have to be blue. But we don't know if Fiona is blue. The statements don't mention Fiona's color.\n\nStatement 11: Kind things are quiet. So if Fiona were kind, she'd be quiet, which she is. But does that mean she's kind? Not necessarily, because the statement is only one way. Just like with Harry, being quiet doesn't automatically make you kind unless you're smart.\n\nWait, no. Harry was smart and kind, so he was quiet. But Fiona is just quiet. So unless she's kind, we can't say she's smart.\n\nIs there any other rule that connects quietness to smartness? Let's see.\n\nStatement 9 again: Quiet, smart things are blue. So if Fiona is smart, she must be blue. But we don't know if she's blue. So if she's not blue, she can't be both quiet and smart. But if she's blue, then she could be smart.\n\nBut we don't have any information about Fiona's color. So we don't know if she's blue or not.\n\nIs there any other way to determine Fiona's smartness? Let's see.\n\nStatement 1: Erin is smart. That doesn't help with Fiona.\n\nStatement 7: Harry is smart. We already used that.\n\nStatement 14: Harry is red because he's smart and blue. But Fiona's color isn't mentioned.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Fiona is quiet. To be smart, she would have to be blue (from statement 9). But we don't know if she's blue. So it's possible she's smart and blue, or she's not smart and not blue. There's no information to confirm or deny either.\n\nTherefore, Fiona's smartness is unknown based on the given information.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown\n\nThe correct option is: C",
