e it's not directly relevant, but it's an additional fact.\n\nWait, but the squirrel is cold, so it sees the rabbit. So, the squirrel sees the rabbit. But the rabbit is chasing the squirrel, so perhaps they are in a mutual chase, but the squirrel is also seeing the rabbit.\n\nBut does this affect the mouse seeing the squirrel? I don't think so. The mouse sees the rabbit, which makes it see the squirrel. So, the mouse sees the squirrel.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any condition that could make the mouse not see the squirrel. For example, if the mouse is cold, it sees the rabbit, which makes it see the squirrel. There's no condition that would prevent that.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the mouse is cold, so it sees the rabbit. Seeing the rabbit means it sees the squirrel. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss any other conditions. Let's go through each statement again.\n\n- The mouse is cold, so it sees the rabbit (statement 8).\n- Seeing the rabbit means it sees the squirrel (statement 9).\n- Therefore, the mouse sees the squirrel.\n\nYes, that seems solid. So, the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe mouse is cold, so it sees the rabbit. Since it sees the rabbit, it must see the squirrel. Therefore, the statement is true.",
