ry is big, green, and smart. From fact 16, since he\'s big and green, he must be furry. So Gary is furry.\n\nNow, from fact 11, all furry people are white. So Gary is white. From fact 13, all white, big people are round. Since Gary is white and big, he must be round. So Gary is round.\n\nNow, from fact 17, if someone is smart and round, then they are quiet. Gary is smart (because he\'s green) and round, so he must be quiet. That aligns with fact 10, which says Gary is quiet. So everything checks out.\n\nWait, but the question is whether Gary is not quiet. Since we\'ve established that Gary is quiet, the statement "Gary is not quiet" is false. So the correct answer should be B) False.\n\nBut let me make sure I didn\'t miss any contradictions. Let\'s go through each fact again to see if anything contradicts Gary being quiet.\n\n- Fact 10: Gary is quiet. So that\'s direct.\n- Fact 14: Quiet implies smart. Gary is smart because he\'s green (fact 18), so that\'s consistent.\n- Fact 15: Smart implies big. So Gary is big.\n- Fact 16: Big and green implies furry. So Gary is furry.\n- Fact 11: Furry implies white. So Gary is white.\n- Fact 13: White and big implies round. So Gary is round.\n- Fact 17: Smart and round implies quiet. So Gary is quiet.\n\nEverything seems to line up without any contradictions. Therefore, the statement "Gary is not quiet" is definitely false.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nThe statement "Gary is not quiet" is false because the given information directly states that Gary is quiet (fact 10). Additionally, through logical deductions, Gary is confirmed to be smart, big, furry, white, and round, all of which consistently support that he is quiet. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nThe correct option is: B) False', '']
