n Charlie is young. Since we've established Charlie is cold, he must be young.\n\nFrom rule 4: All young things are nice. So since Charlie is young, he is nice.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not missing anything. Let me go through each step again.\n\n1. Charlie is quiet \u2192 from rule 1, Charlie is furry.\n2. Charlie is blue and furry \u2192 from rule 7, Charlie is white.\n3. Charlie is white \u2192 from rule 3, Charlie is cold.\n4. Charlie is cold \u2192 from rule 2, Charlie is young.\n5. Charlie is young \u2192 from rule 4, Charlie is nice.\n\nSo that seems to check out. Therefore, Charlie is nice.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any contradiction. For example, Erin is blue, quiet, white, and young. So Erin is white, which makes her cold (from rule 3). She's also young, so she's nice. That fits.\n\nGary is cold, quiet, and young. From rule 1, Gary is quiet \u2192 furry. From rule 5: If Gary is nice, then he's young. But Gary is already young, so that doesn't add much. From rule 6: If something is young, it's nice. So Gary is nice.\n\nDave is blue, cold, and young. From rule 1, since he's not mentioned as quiet, he might not be furry. But wait, rule 1 says all quiet things are furry, but it doesn't say that only quiet things are furry. So Dave could be furry or not. But since he's young, from rule 4, he's nice.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Charlie is nice because he's young, and all young things are nice. So the statement \"Charlie is nice\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
