t chases the squirrel. But statement 10 says the rabbit chases the cow, not the squirrel. So, perhaps the seventh conditional is not directly applicable here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the seventh conditional is triggered because the cat sees the rabbit and the rabbit chases the cat. So, the subject is the cat, which sees the rabbit, and the rabbit chases the cat. Therefore, according to the seventh conditional, the rabbit chases the squirrel. But statement 10 says the rabbit chases the cow, not the squirrel. So, that might be a contradiction, but perhaps the rabbit chases both the cow and the squirrel. However, the given statements don\'t mention the rabbit chasing the squirrel, only the cow and the cat.\n\nWait, statement 10 says the rabbit chases the cow, and statement 9 says the rabbit chases the cat. So, the rabbit chases both the cat and the cow. The seventh conditional says that if something (the cat) sees the rabbit and chases the cat, then the rabbit chases the squirrel. But the cat doesn\'t chase itself, so perhaps the seventh conditional isn\'t applicable here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the subject is the squirrel, which sees the rabbit (statement 15) and chases the cat. But there\'s no statement that the squirrel chases the cat, so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the cat chases the cow (from the second conditional), and the cow chases the cat (from the sixth conditional). The rabbit chases both the cat and the cow. The squirrel is visited by the cow and visits the rabbit, and sees the rabbit.\n\nNow, does the cat chase the rabbit? From the given statements, I don\'t see any direct statement that the cat chases the rabbit. The only chasing involving the cat is that the rabbit chases the cat (statement 9) and the cat chases the cow (from the second conditional). There\'s no statement that the cat chases the rabbit.\n\nHowever, let\'s check if any conditional leads to the cat chasing the rabbit. For example, if the cat chases the cow, doe