icitly stated. However, statement 19 says if something is cold and it chases the cat, then it sees the cat.\n\nWe know the tiger chases the cat (statement 11). Is the tiger cold? From statement 22, big things are cold. We\'ve established the tiger is big (from statement 20 because the dog sees the tiger). So, the tiger is big, therefore it\'s cold (statement 22). So, the tiger is cold.\n\nSo, the tiger is cold and chases the cat. Therefore, according to statement 19, the tiger sees the cat.\n\nSo, the tiger sees the cat.\n\nBut the question is whether the tiger sees the tiger. So, does the tiger see itself?\n\nLooking at the given statements, I don\'t see any direct statement about the tiger seeing itself. However, let\'s see if any conditional statements can help us infer that.\n\nStatement 20: If something sees the tiger, then the tiger is big.\n\nWe know the tiger is big because the dog sees the tiger (statement 5), so statement 20 applies, making the tiger big.\n\nBut does the tiger see itself? There\'s no statement that says the tiger sees the tiger. So, unless we can infer it from other statements, it\'s unknown.\n\nWait, but let\'s think about statement 10: The squirrel sees the dog. Statement 9: The squirrel likes the dog. So, from statement 18: If something likes the squirrel, then it sees the dog. Wait, no, statement 18 says if something likes the squirrel, then it sees the dog. Wait, no, statement 18 is: If something likes the squirrel, then it sees the dog. So, the squirrel likes the dog (statement 9), so according to statement 18, the squirrel sees the dog (which is statement 10, so that\'s consistent).\n\nBut that doesn\'t help with the tiger seeing itself.\n\nAnother angle: Statement 15: If something likes the dog, then it chases the tiger. The tiger likes the dog (statement 13), so the tiger chases the tiger? That seems like a loop, but maybe it\'s just a way to say the tiger chases itself, which is a bit odd, but perhaps it\'s a way to infer that th