messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is green. Charlie is big. Charlie is quiet. Charlie is round. Erin is green. Erin is kind. Erin is nice. Erin is quiet. Fiona is blue. Fiona is kind. Fiona is quiet. If something is kind then it is nice. If something is round and quiet then it is blue. If Charlie is kind then Charlie is big. All big, blue things are kind. Nice things are quiet. All green things are big. Quiet things are kind. Big things are kind. If Anne is green then Anne is round.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Erin is not blue.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '.\n\nThe correct option is: A) True.\n\nWait, no, wait.Let me correct myself.Earlier, I thought it was unknown, but upon re-evaluating, let\'s see.\n\nWait, Erin is green, which makes her big (fact 17).Now, from fact 19, big things are kind, which she is.Now, from fact 15, all big, blue things are kind.So if Erin were blue, she would be kind, which she is.But that does mean she is blue. However, from fact 13, if something is round and noisy, it\'s not blue. Erin is noisy, and we know she\'s round. So if she\'s round, she must be blue. But wait, from fact 20, Anne is red, so she\'s not round. But that\'s only for Anne. So Erin\'s roundness is determined by her being red. Therefore, we can conclude she\'s round, so we can apply fact 13 to her. Therefore, we know she is blue. So the statement "Erin is blue" is known.']]
