 big and rough, then it\'s not nice. But we don\'t know if Fiona is rough. So, Fiona being big and not furry doesn\'t necessarily make her not nice unless she\'s rough. But since we don\'t have information about Fiona\'s roughness, we can\'t conclude she\'s not nice. So, Fiona is nice because she\'s big, unless she\'s rough, which we don\'t know.\n\nBut that doesn\'t affect Dave\'s status. Let me check if any other rules apply to Dave. Since Dave is furry, he\'s big, nice, kind, young, and therefore green. So, all the implications chain together without any contradictions.\n\nWait, another thought: rule 9 says young, big things are green. Since Dave is both young and big, he must be green. That\'s another way to confirm it.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Dave is green. Therefore, the statement "Dave is green" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A', '']
