is big. Therefore, the only way we can conclude Charlie is smart is through statement 15.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not missing anything. Let's go through each statement again.\n\nCharlie is young (1), so he's red (10). Red means furry (8), and furry means green (12). So he's green. Then, red and green means blue (13). So he's blue. Now, blue and green means smart (15). So yes, Charlie is smart.\n\nIs there any contradiction? Let me check. All red things are furry, and all furry are red, so that's consistent. All furry are green, so that's okay. Red and green implies blue, which is okay. Blue and green implies smart, which is okay.\n\nWait, but statement 16 says blue, red things are smart. So Charlie is blue and red, so that also makes him smart. So both statements 15 and 16 apply here, reinforcing that Charlie is smart.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, Charlie is smart. So the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
