Erin is young, but we don't know if she's nice or blue. However, since Erin is green (from point 16 and being white), and point 12 says all green things are blue, so Erin must be blue. So Erin is blue, green, white, and young.\n\nWait, but point 12 says all green things are blue, so since Erin is green, she must be blue. So Erin is blue. Now, point 15 says if Erin is nice and blue, then she is young. We know Erin is young, but we don't know if she's nice. However, since Erin is blue, and point 8 says if something is blue, it's nice, so Erin must be nice. Therefore, Erin is nice, blue, green, white, and young.\n\nNow, let's go back to Charlie. We know Charlie is blue, nice, and kind. We need to determine if Charlie is young or not. The question is whether Charlie is not young.\n\nLooking at the facts, point 13 says if something is young, then it is white. So if Charlie were young, he would have to be white. But Charlie is blue, not white. Therefore, Charlie cannot be young because being young would require him to be white, which contradicts his color being blue.\n\nAlternatively, since Charlie is blue, and point 16 says all white things are green, but Charlie is not green, so he can't be white. Therefore, Charlie can't be young because being young requires being white, which he isn't.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Charlie is not young. Therefore, the statement \"Charlie is not young\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
