le 7, quiet and nice implies rough. So Charlie should be rough. But the context doesn't say that. So perhaps the context is incomplete, but according to the rules, Charlie is rough.\n\nGary: green, young. From rule 4, green implies nice. From rule 5, nice implies quiet. From rule 7, quiet and nice implies rough. So Gary should be rough. But from rule 9, rough and nice implies big. But Gary isn't big, so that's a problem.\n\nWait, perhaps the rules are such that if someone is rough and nice, they must be big, but if they're not big, they can't be both rough and nice. So perhaps Gary can't be both rough and nice because he's not big. But according to rule 4, he's green, so he's nice. And rule 5, he's quiet. So rule 7 says he must be rough. But then rule 9 says he must be big. Since he's not big, perhaps the only way is that he's not rough, but that contradicts rule 7.\n\nThis is a paradox. Maybe I'm missing something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rules don't apply in a way that creates a contradiction. Let me try to see if there's a way for Gary not to be rough without breaking the rules.\n\nIf Gary is not rough, then rule 7 doesn't apply because it's an if-then statement. So if he's not rough, then the antecedent (quiet and nice) is true, but the consequent (rough) is false, which would make the implication false. But in logic, if the antecedent is true and the consequent is false, the implication is false. So that would mean the rule is violated, which can't happen because the rules are given as facts.\n\nTherefore, Gary must be rough, which would require him to be big, but he's not. So perhaps the only way is that Gary is not both quiet and nice, but that's impossible because he's green (nice) and nice implies quiet.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating this. Let me try to see if there's a way for Gary to be rough without being big. But rule 9 says all rough, nice people are big. So if Gary is rough and nice, he must be big. But he's not, so he can't be both rough 