e 6). So that's consistent.\n\nAlso, rule 9: All rough, nice people are big. Gary is rough, nice, so he should be big. But the context says Anne is big, not Gary. So that's a problem. Because according to rule 9, if Gary is rough and nice, he must be big. But the context doesn't say Gary is big. So that's a contradiction.\n\nWait, so if Gary is rough and nice, he must be big. But the context doesn't say Gary is big. So perhaps Gary isn't rough after all. That would resolve the contradiction.\n\nSo maybe I made a mistake earlier. Let me retrace.\n\nGary is green and young. From rule 4, he's nice. From rule 5, he's quiet. From rule 7, quiet and nice implies rough. But if he's rough, then from rule 9, he must be big. But the context doesn't say Gary is big. So that's a problem.\n\nTherefore, perhaps Gary isn't rough. But why? Because if he's not rough, then rule 7 doesn't apply. But rule 7 says if someone is quiet and nice, then they are rough. So if Gary is quiet and nice, he must be rough. But that leads to a contradiction because he's not big.\n\nWait, maybe the rules are such that if someone is rough and nice, they must be big, but if they're not big, they can't be both rough and nice. So perhaps Gary can't be both rough and nice because he's not big. But according to rule 4, he's green, so he's nice. And rule 5, he's quiet. So rule 7 says he must be rough. But then rule 9 says he must be big. Since he's not big, perhaps the only way out is that he's not rough, but that contradicts rule 7.\n\nThis is confusing. Maybe I need to approach it differently.\n\nLet me list all the attributes for each person based on the context and the rules.\n\nAnne: big, rough, young.\n\nBob: young. From rule 6, he's cold. From rule 3: if Bob is rough and green, then he's cold. But Bob is cold regardless because he's young. So Bob's status as rough or green isn't determined yet.\n\nCharlie: cold, green, nice, young. From rule 4, green implies nice. From rule 5, nice implies quiet. From 