The mouse is blue (statement 3), so according to statement 18, the mouse likes the cat. But that doesn\'t affect the cat\'s feelings towards the tiger.\n\nLooking at statement 17: If something is nice, it likes the cat. The mouse is nice (statement 4), so again, the mouse likes the cat. But again, this is about the mouse\'s feelings, not the cat\'s.\n\nStatement 16: If something is blue and visits the cat, it likes the rabbit. The mouse is blue (statement 3) and does it visit the cat? Statement 6 says the mouse visits the tiger. Statement 15 says if something visits the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse. So the mouse visits the tiger, which means the tiger visits the mouse. But does the mouse visit the cat? Statement 12 says if the mouse is big, then it visits the cat. But we don\'t know if the mouse is big. So the mouse might or might not visit the cat. If it does, then according to statement 16, the mouse would like the rabbit. But since the mouse is blue, from statement 18, it already likes the cat. So that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, looking at statement 19: If something is round, it is nice. The mouse is round (statement 5), so it\'s nice (statement 4), which aligns.\n\nStatement 20: If something visits the tiger, it is round. The mouse visits the tiger (statement 6), so the mouse is round (statement 5), which is consistent.\n\nSo, going back to the main question: Does the cat like the tiger? Statement 2 directly says yes. There\'s no conflicting information because the other statements don\'t contradict this. The tiger visiting the cat (statement 11) doesn\'t affect the cat\'s feelings towards the tiger. The fact that the tiger eats the mouse and rabbit doesn\'t impact the cat\'s liking for the tiger either.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, the statement "The cat likes the tiger" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A', '']
