he lion and eats the squirrel, then the squirrel sees the tiger. Wait, no, the squirrel chases the lion and eats the lion (statement 6), not the squirrel. So, the antecedent is: something chases the lion and eats the squirrel. Wait, no, the squirrel chases the lion (statement 5) and the squirrel eats the lion (statement 6). So, the antecedent is the squirrel chases the lion and the squirrel eats the squirrel? That doesn't make sense. Wait, no, the antecedent is \"something chases the lion and it eats the squirrel.\" So, the subject is the same thing. So, if the squirrel chases the lion and the squirrel eats the squirrel, which is impossible because the squirrel can't eat itself. So, perhaps I misapplied statement 13.\n\nWait, maybe I misread statement 13. Let me read it again: \"If something chases the lion and it eats the squirrel then the squirrel sees the tiger.\" So, the subject is the same entity. So, if the same thing chases the lion and eats the squirrel, then the squirrel sees the tiger. But in our case, the squirrel chases the lion (statement 5) and the squirrel eats the lion (statement 6). So, the squirrel chases the lion and eats the lion, not the squirrel. Therefore, the antecedent of statement 13 is not met because the squirrel doesn't eat the squirrel. So, statement 13's condition isn't triggered because the squirrel chases the lion and eats the lion, not the squirrel. Therefore, the squirrel doesn't necessarily see the tiger because of statement 13.\n\nWait, that changes things. So, statement 13 requires that the same thing chases the lion and eats the squirrel. But in our case, the squirrel chases the lion and eats the lion, not the squirrel. So, the antecedent isn't met, so the consequent doesn't apply. Therefore, the squirrel doesn't necessarily see the tiger because of statement 13.\n\nSo, that means the squirrel doesn't see the tiger, so statement 15 doesn't apply. Therefore, the squirrel isn't necessarily blue.\n\nWait, but let's check other ways the squirrel could see the tiger. Statement 14: If the squirrel eats the lion and the squirrel is green, then the lion sees the tiger. The squirrel eats the lion (statement 6) and is green (statement 7), so the antecedent is true. Therefore, the lion sees the tiger. So, the lion sees the tiger.\n\nNow, statement 15 says if something sees the tiger, then it is blue. So, the lion sees the tiger, which means the lion is blue. But the lion's color isn't mentioned elsewhere, so that's possible.\n\nBut does the squirrel see the tiger? From statement 13, we thought it didn't because the antecedent wasn't met. So, the squirrel doesn't see the tiger, so statement 15 doesn't apply to the squirrel. Therefore, the squirrel's color isn't determined by statement 15.\n\nSo, the squirrel is green (statement 7). Is there any other statement that could determine its color? Let's see.\n\nStatement 12: If something is blue, it chases the lion. The squirrel chases the lion (statement 5), so if the squirrel were blue, it would chase the lion, which it does. But that doesn't necessarily mean the squirrel is blue because other things could chase the lion without being blue.\n\nStatement 17: If something chases the dog, then it chases the tiger. The squirrel chases the dog (statement 4), so according to statement 17, the squirrel chases the tiger. So, the squirrel chases the tiger.\n\nNow, does chasing the tiger relate to color? Let's see. If the squirrel chases the tiger, does that imply anything about its color? Not directly, unless another rule connects chasing the tiger to color.\n\nWait, statement 15: If something sees the tiger, then it is blue. But the squirrel doesn't see the tiger, as per our earlier reasoning, so that doesn't apply.\n\nSo, the squirrel is green (statement 7), and there's no statement that says it's blue. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" would be true because the squirrel is green, and unless green and blue can coexist, which isn't specified, the squirrel isn't blue.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that statement 13 might imply the squirrel sees the tiger, but upon re-examining, I realized that the antecedent wasn't met because the squirrel eats the lion, not the squirrel. So, the squirrel doesn't see the tiger, so statement 15 doesn't apply, meaning the squirrel isn't necessarily blue.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel is green, and there's no information that it's blue. So, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything. Let's go through all the statements again.\n\n- The squirrel is green (statement 7).\n- The squirrel is rough (statement 8).\n- Rough things are big (statement 16), so the squirrel is big.\n- The squirrel chases the dog (statement 4) and the lion (statement 5).\n- The squirrel eats the lion (statement 6).\n- The squirrel sees the dog (statement 9) and the lion (statement 10).\n- The tiger is rough (statement 11), so the tiger is big.\n- The lion sees the dog (statement 3) and, from statement 14, the lion sees the tiger because the squirrel eats the lion and is green.\n- The lion sees the tiger, so according to statement 15, the lion is blue.\n- The squirrel chases the tiger (from statement 17 because it chases the dog).\n\nSo, the squirrel chases the tiger. Does that imply anything about its color? Not directly unless another rule applies.\n\nWait, statement 12: If something is blue, it chases the lion. The squirrel chases the lion, but that doesn't mean it's blue because other things can chase the lion without being blue.\n\nSo, the squirrel is green, and there's no statement that says it's blue. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" is true.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that statement 13 might imply the squirrel sees the tiger, but upon re-examining, I realized that the antecedent wasn't met because the squirrel eats the lion, not the squirrel. So, the squirrel doesn't see the tiger, so statement 15 doesn't apply, meaning the squirrel isn't necessarily blue.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel is green, and there's no information that it's blue. So, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me check if the squirrel could be blue for any other reason. For example, if the squirrel chases the tiger, does that imply anything? Statement 12 says if something is blue, it chases the lion. But the squirrel chases the lion, so if it were blue, that's consistent. But the converse isn't necessarily true\u2014chasing the lion doesn't imply being blue.\n\nSo, the squirrel is green, and there's no evidence it's blue. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe squirrel is green, and there's no information indicating it's blue. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" is true.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
