e might not be cold. Therefore, point 14 doesn\'t necessarily apply to Dave because he\'s not cold. So that doesn\'t affect his redness.\n\nWait, but point 14 says young, cold people are red. So if someone is young and cold, they are red. But Dave is young (because he\'s big) but not necessarily cold. So Dave being red doesn\'t depend on being cold; he\'s red regardless because point 6 says he is. So that\'s fine.\n\nAnother point to consider: point 17 says if Anne is kind and cold, then she\'s smart. But Anne is rough and cold, so point 16 says all kind people are rough, but Anne is rough, so she must be kind? Wait, no, point 16 says all kind people are rough, but it doesn\'t say that all rough people are kind. So Anne is rough, but she might not be kind. Wait, but point 17 says if Anne is kind and cold, then she\'s smart. But Anne is cold and rough. So if she\'s kind, then she\'s smart. But from point 16, since she\'s rough, she might be kind, but it\'s not necessarily the case. Hmm, this might not directly affect Dave, though.\n\nWait, let\'s get back to Dave. He\'s big, so young. Young people are kind (point 18), so Dave is kind. Kind people are rough (point 16), so Dave is rough. Rough people are big (point 15), which is consistent. So Dave is rough, big, young, kind, red, and smart. All these attributes seem to fit together without any contradictions.\n\nWait, but let me check if being red is only for young, cold people. Point 14 says young, cold people are red. But Dave is young but not cold, so does that mean he can\'t be red? Or does point 14 only say that young, cold people are red, but doesn\'t say that only young, cold people are red. So maybe there are other ways to be red. For example, point 6 directly states that Dave is red, so that\'s a fact regardless of other rules. So even if point 14 says young, cold people are red, it doesn\'t exclude others from being red. So Dave can be red without being cold.\n\nWait, but point 14 says "Young, cold people ar