ds itself? That doesn't make much sense. Maybe I'm misunderstanding. Let me re-examine statement 14: \"If something is big, then it needs the squirrel.\" So, if the squirrel is big, then it needs the squirrel. That seems a bit circular, but perhaps it's just a way to say that big things need the squirrel, regardless of who they are.\n\nMoving on, statement 15: If something visits the squirrel and it visits the lion, then it needs the squirrel. So, if an entity visits both the squirrel and the lion, it needs the squirrel.\n\nStatement 19: If something is big and it visits the lion, then the lion needs the squirrel. Since the squirrel is big (from statement 13), if the squirrel visits the lion, then the lion needs the squirrel.\n\nBut wait, statement 9 says the squirrel does not need the lion. So, if the squirrel is big and visits the lion, then the lion needs the squirrel. But the squirrel doesn't need the lion, which might not directly affect this.\n\nNow, let's see if the squirrel visits the lion. If the squirrel does visit the lion, then according to statement 15, since the squirrel visits both the squirrel (itself) and the lion, it would need the squirrel. But that's a bit redundant because the squirrel is the same as itself. Maybe that's not the right way to look at it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps another entity visits both the squirrel and the lion. Let's see who else visits the squirrel. From statement 10, the squirrel visits the bald eagle, but does anyone else visit the squirrel? The context doesn't mention anyone else visiting the squirrel except the squirrel itself, which doesn't make sense. So maybe statement 15 isn't applicable here because no one else is visiting both the squirrel and the lion.\n\nWait, the lion visits the tiger (statement 5), and the tiger visits the bald eagle (statement 12). The squirrel visits the bald eagle (statement 10). So, the squirrel and the tiger both visit the bald eagle. But does the squirrel visit the lion? That's what we'r