ct 13, if someone is blue and kind, they are young. So Gary is young.\n\nBob:\n\n- Bob is quiet and young (facts 2,3). From fact 10, being quiet implies furry, so Bob is furry. From fact 16, young people are kind, so Bob is kind.\n\nWait, but Bob is quiet, so from fact 12, he's young. From fact 16, he's kind. So Bob is kind, quiet, young, and furry.\n\nNow, going back to Anne. She's smart \u2192 furry \u2192 quiet \u2192 young \u2192 kind \u2192 rough. So yes, Anne is rough.\n\nIs there any contradiction? Let me see. If Anne is rough, then from fact 8, since she's kind (from fact 16), she should be blue. So Anne is blue. But does that affect anything else? Let's see.\n\nIf Anne is blue and kind, from fact 13, she must be young, which we already know. So no contradiction there.\n\nFiona: She's not quiet (fact 4). From fact 12, quiet people are young, so Fiona might not be young. If she's not young, from fact 16, she might not be kind. But fact 15 says if Fiona is blue, then she's not kind. So if Fiona is blue, she's not kind. But if she's not young, she might not be kind either. So Fiona's status as blue or not isn't directly affecting Anne.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Anne is rough. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
