ely, perhaps it's a mistake, and it should be \"it sees the bear.\" But regardless, I'll proceed with what's given.\n\nNow, looking at statement 21: If something is red, then it needs the bear. The bald eagle is red (statement 4), so according to statement 21, the bald eagle needs the bear. So, the bald eagle needs the bear.\n\nNow, going back to statement 20: If something is red and it needs the bear, then it is big. The bald eagle is red (statement 4) and needs the bear (from statement 21), so according to statement 20, the bald eagle is big.\n\nWait, that seems to directly answer the question. So, the bald eagle is big because it's red and needs the bear, which makes it big per statement 20.\n\nBut let me double-check to make sure there are no contradictions.\n\nThe bald eagle is young (statement 5) and red (statement 4), so from statement 13, it's green (statement 3). That's consistent.\n\nFrom statement 21, since the bald eagle is red, it needs the bear. So, the bald eagle needs the bear.\n\nFrom statement 20, since the bald eagle is red and needs the bear, it must be big.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The bald eagle is big\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any other rule that might affect this. For example, statement 16: If something is big and chases the rabbit, then it sees the bear. The bald eagle chases the rabbit (statement 1) and is big (from above), so according to statement 16, it should see the bear. But does the bald eagle see the bear?\n\nFrom statement 7: The bear sees the bald eagle. That doesn't necessarily mean the bald eagle sees the bear. So, is there any statement that says the bald eagle sees the bear?\n\nLooking at statement 18: If something chases the rabbit, then it sees the bald eagle. The chaser is the bald eagle, so the bald eagle sees the bald eagle, which is itself. So, that doesn't help.\n\nIs there any other statement that says the bald eagle sees the bear? Let me check.\n\nStatement 6: The bald eagle sees the rabbit. So, the bald eagle sees the rabbit, but not necessarily the bear.\n\nSo, according to statement 16, if the bald eagle is big and chases the rabbit, it should see the bear. But we don't have any information that the bald eagle sees the bear. So, is there a contradiction here?\n\nWait, if the bald eagle is big (from statement 20), and it chases the rabbit (statement 1), then according to statement 16, it should see the bear. But we don't have any information that the bald eagle sees the bear. So, is this a problem?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the statement 16 is a conditional, meaning that if it's big and chases the rabbit, then it must see the bear. But if it doesn't see the bear, then it can't be big. But we have from statement 20 that it is big because it's red and needs the bear. So, there's a conflict here.\n\nWait, let me re-examine statement 16: If something is big and it chases the rabbit, then it sees the bear. So, if the bald eagle is big and chases the rabbit, then it must see the bear. But we don't know if the bald eagle sees the bear. So, does that mean that the assumption that the bald eagle is big is incorrect because it doesn't see the bear?\n\nBut from statement 20, we concluded that the bald eagle is big because it's red and needs the bear. So, there's a contradiction here. Therefore, perhaps the initial conclusion that the bald eagle is big is incorrect because it doesn't see the bear, which would violate statement 16 if it's big.\n\nWait, but statement 16 is a conditional. It doesn't say that if something is big and chases the rabbit, it must see the bear; rather, it's a rule that if it's big and chases the rabbit, then it sees the bear. So, if the bald eagle is big and chases the rabbit, then it must see the bear. But we don't know if it sees the bear. So, perhaps the bald eagle is not big because it doesn't see the bear, which would mean that statement 20's conclusion that it's big is incorrect.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle does see the be