arily need the lion unless stated otherwise.\n\nWait, premise 6 says the rabbit needs the lion. So, the rabbit depends on the lion. So, the rabbit needs the lion, and the rabbit also needs the bald eagle (premise 5). So, the rabbit has two dependencies: lion and bald eagle.\n\nNow, going back to premise 10: If something needs the lion and visits the rabbit, then it chases the lion. So, does the tiger need the lion? We don\'t have any premise stating that the tiger needs the lion. The only dependencies we know are:\n\n- Lion needs rabbit (premise 3)\n- Rabbit needs lion and bald eagle (premises 5 and 6)\n- Tiger visits rabbit (premise 7)\n\nSo, the tiger doesn\'t seem to need the lion. Therefore, premise 10 might not apply to the tiger because the tiger doesn\'t need the lion. So, perhaps premise 10 doesn\'t lead us anywhere yet.\n\nLet\'s look at premise 11: If something is big, then it chases the lion. We know the bald eagle is big (from premise 8). So, the bald eagle chases the lion? Wait, does the bald eagle chase the lion? Let me check the premises. Premise 1 says the bald eagle chases the rabbit, premise 2 says it chases the tiger. There\'s no mention of the bald eagle chasing the lion. So, according to premise 11, since the bald eagle is big, it should chase the lion. But we don\'t have any premise stating that the bald eagle chases the lion. Hmm, that\'s a contradiction. Or perhaps I\'m missing something.\n\nWait, premise 11 says if something is big, then it chases the lion. So, the bald eagle is big, so it must chase the lion. But in the given premises, the bald eagle chases the rabbit and the tiger, but not the lion. So, is this a problem? Or perhaps the premises are consistent because the bald eagle chases the lion as well, but it\'s not explicitly stated. Wait, no, the premises only mention the bald eagle chasing the rabbit and the tiger. So, according to premise 11, the bald eagle should chase the lion, but it\'s not mentioned. So, perhaps this is an oversight, or maybe I\'m misapplying the rules.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle does chase the lion, but it\'s not explicitly stated. So, perhaps we can infer that the bald eagle chases the lion because it\'s big. So, that would be an additional fact.\n\nIf the bald eagle chases the lion, then from premise 14: If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit. So, if the bald eagle chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit.\n\nBut from premise 4, the rabbit does not chase the lion. So, the lion chasing the rabbit would mean that the lion is chasing the rabbit, but the rabbit isn\'t chasing the lion. That seems possible.\n\nWait, but premise 14 says if X chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit. So, if the bald eagle chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit. So, that would mean the lion chases the rabbit.\n\nBut does the lion chase the rabbit? Let\'s see. From premise 3, the lion needs the rabbit. So, perhaps the lion chases the rabbit to get it, but premise 4 says the rabbit does not chase the lion. So, the rabbit doesn\'t chase the lion, but the lion might chase the rabbit.\n\nWait, but if the lion chases the rabbit, then from premise 8, since the lion chases the rabbit, the lion must be big. So, the lion is big.\n\nNow, if the lion is big, from premise 11, it chases the lion. Wait, that would mean the lion chases itself. Because premise 11 says if something is big, it chases the lion. So, if the lion is big, then it chases the lion, which is itself.\n\nSo, that would imply that the lion chases the lion, which is the statement we\'re evaluating.\n\nBut let\'s make sure this chain of reasoning is correct.\n\n1. The bald eagle chases the rabbit (premise 1) → bald eagle is big (premise 8).\n2. Since the bald eagle is big (premise 11) → it chases the lion.\n3. If the bald eagle chases the lion (premise 14) → the lion chases the rabbit.\n4. The lion chases the rabbit → from premise 8, the lion is big.\n5. Since the lion is big (pre