ion, then the lion does not need the rabbit. But since the lion does need the rabbit, the antecedent must be false. So, either the lion does not chase the rabbit, or it does visit the lion. But we\'ve already established that the lion chases the rabbit, so it must visit the lion. So, the lion visits itself, which is a bit odd, but perhaps it\'s acceptable for the sake of the problem.\n\nAnother point: premise 12 says if something chases the tiger, then the tiger needs the rabbit. The bald eagle chases the tiger (premise 2), so the tiger needs the rabbit (premise 12). That\'s consistent with premise 7, where the tiger visits the rabbit. So, the tiger needs the rabbit and visits it, which makes sense.\n\nPremise 13 says if something visits the tiger, then the tiger is not green. Since the tiger visits the rabbit (premise 7), does that mean the tiger is not green? Wait, premise 7 says the tiger visits the rabbit, not that something visits the tiger. So, premise 13 would apply if, say, the rabbit visits the tiger, but premise 7 says the tiger visits the rabbit. So, premise 13 doesn\'t directly apply here unless the rabbit visits the tiger, which isn\'t stated.\n\nWait, premise 7 is "The tiger visits the rabbit." So, the subject is the tiger, and the object is the rabbit. So, premise 13 says if something visits the tiger, then the tiger is not green. So, if X visits the tiger, then the tiger is not green. But in premise 7, the tiger is visiting the rabbit, not being visited. So, premise 13 doesn\'t apply here. Therefore, the tiger\'s color isn\'t determined by premise 13 in this context.\n\nSo, going back, the main chain of reasoning seems solid:\n\n- Bald eagle chases rabbit → bald eagle is big.\n- Bald eagle is big → chases lion.\n- Bald eagle chases lion → lion chases rabbit.\n- Lion chases rabbit → lion is big.\n- Lion is big → chases lion.\n\nTherefore, the lion chases the lion, making the statement true.\n\nI don\'t see any contradictions in this reasoning. All the