 Harry cannot be young because he\'s not cold. So Harry is green, not cold, not young, but still furry because green people are furry.\n\nGary is big and furry (points 4 and 5). From point 10, being furry makes him green. So Gary is green. From point 16, if Gary is green, then he\'s furry, which is consistent because he already is furry.\n\nHarry is green, so from point 11, he\'s white. Also, since he\'s green and not cold, point 12 says if someone is green and cold, they\'re white. But Harry isn\'t cold, so that rule doesn\'t apply to him, but he\'s still white because of point 11.\n\nWait, but let me go back to Bob. Bob is cold, so he\'s furry (point 15). Being furry makes him green (point 10). So yes, Bob is green. So the statement should be true.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn\'t miss anything. Is there any rule that could make Bob not green? Let\'s see. All the rules seem to support that if someone is cold, they\'re furry, and if they\'re furry, they\'re green. So Bob being cold leads to him being green.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Bob is green. Therefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A', '']
