tatement 12 says the squirrel likes the lion, not the other way around.\n\nWait, statement 17 says if something likes the squirrel and needs the squirrel, then the squirrel sees the cow. So, we need to find an entity that likes the squirrel and needs the squirrel.\n\nLooking at the given information:\n\n- The lion likes the squirrel? Wait, no. Statement 9 says the lion likes the bald eagle. Statement 12 says the squirrel likes the lion. So, the squirrel likes the lion, but the lion doesn't necessarily like the squirrel.\n\nWait, statement 13: If something sees the lion, then the lion likes the squirrel. So, if something sees the lion, the lion likes the squirrel. But does the lion see the squirrel? Statement 11 says the lion sees the squirrel. So, according to statement 13, since the lion sees itself (wait, no, the lion sees the squirrel), then the lion likes the squirrel. So, from statement 13, because the lion sees the squirrel, the lion likes the squirrel.\n\nSo, the lion likes the squirrel (from statement 13 and 11). Now, does the lion need the squirrel? Statement 10 says the lion needs the squirrel. So, the lion likes the squirrel and needs the squirrel.\n\nNow, applying statement 17: If something likes the squirrel and it needs the squirrel, then the squirrel sees the cow. Since the lion likes the squirrel (from statement 13) and needs the squirrel (statement 10), then the squirrel sees the cow.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel sees the cow. So, the statement \"The squirrel does not see the cow\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The squirrel sees the cow because the lion likes and needs the squirrel, which triggers statement 17, leading to the squirrel seeing the cow. So, the squirrel does see the cow, making the statement \"The squirrel does not see the cow\" false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False.",
