ow is young, if it's also cold, it would eat itself, which doesn't make sense. Alternatively, maybe the lion is young and cold, so it would eat the cow. But we don't know if the lion is cold.\n\nNow, let's try to piece this together. The main question is whether the bear eats the lion. From statement 1, the bear eats the cow. The cow eats the squirrel (statement 3). The lion is red (statement 6) and young (statement 7). The lion visits the cow (statement 8), and the cow visits the lion (statement 5). \n\nLooking at statement 16: If something is red, then it eats the lion. The lion is red, so does that mean the lion eats itself? That can't be. Alternatively, maybe another red entity eats the lion. But the only red entity is the lion itself. So, perhaps this statement doesn't apply here because it would lead to a contradiction.\n\nStatement 18 says if something eats the lion, then it sees the cow. So, if the bear eats the lion, then the bear would see the cow. But from statement 1, the bear already eats the cow, so seeing the cow is already established. But does the bear see the cow? Statement 17 says if the bear visits the squirrel and is round, then it sees the cow. But we don't know if the bear is round or visits the squirrel beyond the squirrel visiting the bear.\n\nWait, statement 10 says the squirrel visits the bear, so the squirrel is with the bear. Does that mean the bear sees the squirrel? It's possible, but not explicitly stated. If the bear sees the squirrel, then from statement 13, since the bear is blue and sees the squirrel, the squirrel would be round. But we don't know if the squirrel is round or not.\n\nFrom statement 14, if something sees the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the cow. The squirrel sees the bear (statement 9), but does the bear see the squirrel? If yes, then the squirrel would eat the cow. But the cow already eats the squirrel (statement 3), which is a contradiction. Therefore, perhaps the bear does not see the squirrel, so the conditi