d make the statement \"Harry is young\" true.\n\nBut hold on, let's check if there's any contradiction. From fact 13: If something is young, then it's white. Erin is young (fact 5), so Erin must be white. But from fact 11: If Erin is green, then she's white. But we don't know if Erin is green. However, from fact 16, all white things are green, so Erin being white (from fact 13) would make her green. So Erin is green, which from fact 11, if she's green, she's white, which is consistent.\n\nWait, but Erin is young, so she's white (fact 13). From fact 16, she's green. So Erin is green, white, and young. Now, from fact 15: If Erin is nice and blue, then she's young. But we don't know if Erin is nice or blue. From fact 4, Charlie is nice. From fact 8, Charlie is blue, so he's nice. But Erin's niceness isn't directly stated. However, since all nice things are kind (fact 10), and Erin is young, which makes her white and green, but we don't have info on her being nice or blue.\n\nWait, but let's focus back on Harry. Harry is white, so from fact 14, he's young. So the statement should be true. But let me make sure there's no contradiction.\n\nHarry is white, so he's green (fact 16). Being green, he's blue (fact 12). Being blue, he's nice (fact 8). Being nice, he's kind (fact 10), which matches fact 6. So all these are consistent.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Harry is young\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
