f Harry is round, then Harry is big. Harry is round (point 10) and big (from point 17, since he's round). Harry is also rough (point 9) and young (point 11). So Harry is a rough, young, big, round, white thing? Wait, let's check.\n\nHarry is rough (9), round (10), young (11). From point 16, since Harry is big (from point 17, because he's round), he's white. So Harry is rough, white, and young. From point 12, all rough, white things are young, which fits Harry.\n\nNow, looking back at Dave. Dave is big, so he's white (16). He's round (13), green (15), red (20). Does he have any other attributes? We don't know if he's rough or not.\n\nPoint 14: All white, red things are rough. Dave is white and red, so he must be rough.\n\nSo now, Dave is big, white, red, rough, round, green. Now, does being rough and young imply anything? Point 18 says all rough, young things are red, which Dave is red, but that doesn't confirm he's young.\n\nPoint 19: If something is young, it's green. Dave is green, but that doesn't mean he's young because other things can be green without being young.\n\nPoint 12: All rough, white things are young. Dave is rough and white, so according to this rule, he must be young.\n\nWait, that's a key point. Since Dave is rough and white, point 12 says all rough, white things are young. Therefore, Dave must be young.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Dave is big \u2192 white (16)\n- Dave is big \u2192 round (13)\n- Dave is round \u2192 green (15)\n- Dave is green \u2192 red (20)\n- Dave is big and white \u2192 rough (14)\n- Dave is rough and white \u2192 young (12)\n\nTherefore, Dave is young.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A) True\n\nThe statement \"Dave is young\" is true because Dave is both rough and white, which according to the given rules, means he must be young. \n\nThe correct option is: A",
