affect him.\n\nRule 17: If something is rough and green, then it is red. Again, Charlie isn't green, so this doesn't apply.\n\nRule 19: If Erin is red, then Erin is green. Erin is green (point 6), so if Erin were red, she would be green, which she already is. But Erin is white (point 7), so she's not red. So this rule doesn't affect Charlie.\n\nRule 20: All red, rough things are quiet. Fiona is red (point 11) and rough (since she's green, per rule 18, and green implies rough). So Fiona is red, rough, and quiet, which fits this rule.\n\nRule 21: If something is quiet and not red, then it is not white. Let's see who is quiet. Fiona is quiet (point 10). She is red (point 11), so this rule doesn't apply to her. Erin is not mentioned as quiet, so maybe she isn't. Anne is kind, but not mentioned as quiet. Charlie isn't mentioned as quiet either. So, if someone is quiet and not red, they can't be white. But since Charlie isn't quiet (as far as we know), this rule doesn't affect him.\n\nNow, looking back at Charlie's attributes: not big, not green, and white. Are there any rules that could contradict this?\n\nRule 15: If something is kind, then it is green. Anne is kind (point 1), so she must be green. But Anne's color isn't mentioned, so maybe that's an oversight, but it doesn't affect Charlie.\n\nRule 13: If Erin is big and red, then she's kind. Erin is big (point 5) and white (point 7), so she's not red. Therefore, the condition isn't met, so the conclusion doesn't apply.\n\nSo, going back to Charlie, all the rules seem to support that he is white. There's no contradiction because he's not green, not big, and the rules don't impose any conflicting conditions on him. Therefore, the statement \"Charlie is white\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
