an't determine his age.\n\nWait, but let's think about the fact that all cold, nice things are young (rule 18). So, if Charlie were cold and nice, he'd be young. But we don't know if he's cold or nice.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie were not cold, then rule 18 doesn't apply. But we don't know if he's cold.\n\nWait, but if Charlie were white, he'd be cold (rule 15). If he's cold and nice, he's young. But we don't know if he's nice.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie were blue and cold, he'd be nice (rule 14). If he's nice and cold, he's young (rule 18). But we don't know if he's blue or cold.\n\nSo, in all cases, we don't have enough information to determine Charlie's age. Therefore, the statement \"Charlie is young\" is unknown.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown\n\nCharlie's age cannot be determined based on the given information. While we know Charlie is big, there's no information about his color, niceness, or other attributes that could help determine his age. Therefore, it's unknown whether Charlie is young.",
