ent 9, if something sees the rabbit, it sees the squirrel. So, the mouse sees the squirrel. So, the mouse sees both the rabbit and the squirrel.\n\nNow, statement 10: If something is cold and it sees the squirrel, then the squirrel is cold. The mouse is cold and sees the squirrel, so according to statement 10, the squirrel is cold.\n\nWait, that's a key point. So, the squirrel is cold because the mouse is cold and sees the squirrel. Therefore, the squirrel is cold.\n\nBut the question is whether the squirrel is not cold. So, if the squirrel is cold, then the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The mouse is cold, sees the squirrel, so the squirrel is cold. So, the squirrel is cold. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn't miss anything. Let's go through the chain again.\n\n1. Mouse is cold (statement 2).\n2. Mouse sees rabbit (statement 8).\n3. Mouse sees squirrel (statement 9, because mouse sees rabbit, so it sees squirrel).\n4. Since mouse is cold and sees squirrel, squirrel is cold (statement 10).\n\nYes, that seems correct. So, the squirrel is cold, making the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any contradiction. For example, does the squirrel chasing the lion affect its temperature? The squirrel is chasing the lion, but there's no statement that says chasing makes you cold or not. The only way the squirrel's temperature is determined is through the mouse seeing it, which makes it cold.\n\nSo, the squirrel is cold, so the statement is false.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nThe squirrel is cold because the mouse, being cold, sees the squirrel, which according to the given rules, makes the squirrel cold. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false.",
